Desc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; San Juan River Basin","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.297cdc701262bb8a1e8beb22ff344aed20ab83bb.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.297cdc701262bb8a1e8beb22ff344aed20ab83bb.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 25 at 2:21PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nIn the wake of today's precipitation, our attention will turn to the\nEastern Pacific. Tropical moisture is anticipated to spread across\nthe Desert Southwest early next week, drawing moisture from a pair of\nHurricanes; Odalys and Polo. The forecast becomes increasing complex\ndue to a closed low currently forecast to drive across the Southwest\nCONUS as Hurricane Polo approaches the Baja California Peninsula.\nStrong southerly winds ahead of the low are expected to pull tropical\nmoisture towards eastern Utah and western Colorado as early as Monday\nmorning. The challenge and uncertainty in our precipitation forecast\nincrease as the week progresses and the low traverses our southern\nStates. Excessive rainfall is most probable over AZ and NM, attm, but\nthe northerly extent poses a threat to our southern San Juan river\nbasins. Current forecasts suggests 1 to 3 inches of rain over Monday\nmorning through Wednesday. At face value, rivers and tributaries will\nsee a bump up due to said moisture. However, localized heavier\namounts could result in minor flooding across the southern San Juan\nfoothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been observed over the\nprevious 7 days. However, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts\n(i.e. above 90th percentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action\nstage to minor flooding. Most probable basins to see significant\nrises include; the Rio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito\nCreek, and the Animas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Please continue to monitor the forecast as these systems\nevolve. Especially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in\nthe southwest quadrant of the forecast area.","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-29T20:16:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 292016 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 216 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered to widespread precipitation showers and storms continue. - Flooding concerns are increased for rain-soaked terrain, especially within and adjacent to the southern San Juans. - A Flood Watch is in effect for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado t","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-29T19:57:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION.. Rain and thunderstorms are expected to continue through Wednesday evening, primarily south of I-70. Prolonged or heavy periods of rainfall could lead to flooding, especially in areas that have recently received substantial rain. By Thursday high pressure begins to move into the area, leading to drier and warmer conditions. However, enough moisture remains to keep critical fire weathe","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":null,"aviation":{"icao":"KGUC","distance_km":35.3,"raw_metar":"METAR KGUC 291956Z AUTO 00000KT 10SM CLR 17/06 A3002 RMK AO2 SLP095 T01670061 $","temp_f":62,"wind_dir":0,"wind_kt":0,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"CLR","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1016.7,"observed":1790711760,"raw_taf":"TAF KGUC 291728Z 2918/3018 VRB04KT P6SM SCT040 BKN060 PROB30 2921/2924 -SHRA BKN040 FM300000 VRB04KT P6SM VCSH SCT050 OVC090 PROB30 3000/3006 -SHRA SCT050 OVC070 FM300600 03006KT P6SM -SHRA BKN045 OVC070","url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KGUC&hours=0"},"airgradient":null,"airnow":{"aqi":6,"category":"Good","parameter":"PM10","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-29 13","by_parameter":{"PM2.5":{"aqi":5,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-29 13"},"PM10":{"aqi":6,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-29 13"}},"url":"https://www.airnow.gov/","distance_km":8},"openmeteo_aq":{"pm25":1,"pm10":1.4,"co":99,"no2":0.1,"so2":0.2,"o3":83,"european_aqi":32,"us_aqi":27,"time":"2026-09-29T14:00"},"usgs":{"station_id":"09124500","station_name":"LAKE FORK AT GATEVIEW, CO.","distance_km":19.1,"discharge_cfs":169,"gauge_height_ft":1.82,"water_temp_f":55,"observed":"2026-09-29T14:15:00.000-06:00","url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/nwis/uv?site_no=09124500"},"snotel":null,"cdot_camera":{"id":"9286","name":"US 50 MP 135.50 EB at CR 26 Blue Mesa Reservoir","lat":38.464993,"lon":-107.26362,"distance_km":6.3,"imageUrl":null,"pageUrl":"https://maps.cotrip.org/?lat=38.464993&lon=-107.26362&zoom=12"},"cdot_rwis":null,"cdot_roads":{"updatedAt":"2026-09-29T20:48:43.519Z","alerts":[{"id":"OpenTMS-Incident35466277999","title":"Maintenance Operations","type":"Maintenance Operations","description":"Between 65.3 Road and US 550; Maple Street (Montrose) from Mi